 \section{Related Work}
 
+
 \subsection{Concept and Data Drift}
-Distributional change in streaming data is separated into covariate shift, where
-$P(X)$ changes while $P(Y\mid X)$ is stable, and concept drift, where $P(Y\mid X)$
-itself changes \cite{gama2014survey,lu2018learning}. Survey work documents a long
-history of taxonomies and adaptation strategies \cite{khamassi2018discussion},
-beginning with the recurring concepts of STAGGER \cite{schlimmer1986incremental} and
-the robustness--flexibility trade-off studied by Widmer \cite{widmer1994combining}.
-The two call for different responses, since covariate shift can often be absorbed by
-recalibrating a decision boundary whereas concept drift invalidates the learned
-mapping. Recurring drift has received less attention than gradual or abrupt drift
-despite being common in systems whose load follows periodic patterns
-\cite{gomes2017adaptive}.
+Drift in a data stream is usually sorted into two cases. Under covariate shift the
+marginal $P(X)$ changes while $P(Y\mid X)$ holds, and under concept drift it is the
+conditional $P(Y\mid X)$ that changes \cite{gama2004learning,lu2018learning}. Surveys
+have catalogued the taxonomies and adaptation strategies built up over the years
+\cite{khamassi2018discussion}. The story starts with STAGGER and its recurring concepts
+\cite{schlimmer1986incremental}, and with Widmer's analysis of robustness against
+flexibility \cite{widmer1994combining}. 
+
 
 \subsection{Drift Detection}
-Detectors trigger adaptation rather than performing it. ADWIN compares sub-window
-means over an adaptive window \cite{bifet2007learning}; Page-Hinkley is a sequential
-cumulative-sum procedure \cite{page1954continuous}; DDM and EDD monitor the error rate
-and its variance \cite{gama2004learning,baena2006early}, while RDDM adds reduced false
-alarms \cite{barros2017rddm} and EDMA and EWMA charts track an exponentially weighted
-moving average of the error \cite{ross2012exponentially}. Label-free detectors test the feature stream instead,
-using Hoeffding bounds \cite{frias2014online}, an incremental Kolmogorov--Smirnov
-statistic \cite{dos2016fast}, or the earth-mover distance \cite{brockhoff2020time},
-with lightweight variants for constrained IoT deployments \cite{9427288}. They
-differ in sensitivity and firing frequency, which translates into adaptation cost.
+
+
+ADWIN compares sub-window means inside an adaptive window \cite{bifet2007learning},
+and Page-Hinkley is a sequential cumulative-sum procedure \cite{page1954continuous}.
+DDM and EDD follow the error rate together with its variance
+\cite{gama2004learning,baena2006early}. RDDM was designed to give fewer false alarms
+\cite{barros2017rddm}, while the EDMA and EWMA charts track an exponentially weighted
+moving average of the error \cite{ross2012exponentially}. A separate family needs no
+labels and tests the features directly. Examples use Hoeffding bounds
+\cite{frias2014online}, an incremental Kolmogorov--Smirnov statistic
+\cite{dos2016fast}, or the earth-mover distance \cite{brockhoff2020time}, and
+lightweight versions exist for constrained IoT deployments \cite{9427288}. 
+
 
 \subsection{Adaptive and Online Learning}
-Online ensembles adapt by updating constituent models as data arrives. Dynamic
-weighted majority weights learners by recent accuracy and adds or removes them as the
-stream evolves \cite{JMLR:v8:kolter07a}, an idea disagreement-minimizing methods had
-already applied to drifting concepts \cite{helmbold1994tracking}; adaptive random
-forests extend online bagging with drift-aware tree replacement \cite{gomes2017adaptive},
-the accuracy-updated ensemble reweights components under drift \cite{brzezinski2013reacting},
-and such designs have been specialised to social big-data and IoT streams
-\cite{abbasi2021elstream,yang2021pwpae}. Single-pass incremental learners such as
-Hoeffding trees bound memory and update cost per example \cite{domingos2000mining},
-and the River library implements the detectors and incremental learners used here
-\cite{montiel2021river}. Incremental updating changes \emph{how} a model is updated
-rather than whether an already-adequate model can be retrieved;
-Section~\ref{sec:ablation} reports a comparison in which it did not improve the
-accuracy--cost trade-off on our stream.
+Online ensembles handle drift by updating their members as new data arrives. Dynamic
+weighted majority weights each learner by its recent accuracy and adds or drops
+learners along the way \cite{JMLR:v8:kolter07a}; it grew out of earlier methods that
+minimize disagreement on drifting concepts \cite{helmbold1994tracking}. Adaptive random
+forests pair online bagging with drift-aware tree replacement
+\cite{gomes2017adaptive}, and the accuracy-updated ensemble reweights its components
+when drift appears \cite{brzezinski2013reacting}. Similar designs have been proposed
+for social big-data and IoT streams \cite{abbasi2021elstream,yang2021pwpae}.
+Single-pass incremental learners, of which Hoeffding trees are the standard example,
+keep memory use and per-example update cost bounded \cite{domingos2000mining}. The
+detectors and incremental learners in our experiments come from the River library
+\cite{montiel2021river}. All of this work is about \emph{how} a model gets
+updated. None of it considers fetching a model that is already good enough. In
+Section~\ref{sec:ablation} we test one incremental learner against our approach, and
+on our stream it does not improve the accuracy--cost trade-off.
+
 
 \subsection{Transfer under Recurring Conditions}
-Transfer learning moves representations or parameters from a source task to a
-related target task \cite{pan2010transfer}. Retaining rather than discarding learned
-structure has an older lineage in work on the role of forgetting \cite{markovitch1988role}
-and the cost of reusing compiled knowledge chunks \cite{tambe1988some}, both
-observing that relearning what is already known is wasteful. RAPT transfers something
-narrower: a previously fitted policy and its regime identity are stored and retrieved
-whole, so the decision is not how to adapt but whether adaptation is needed at all;
-without recurrence the mechanism degenerates to ordinary retraining.
+Transfer learning moves representations or parameters learned on a source task over to
+a related target task \cite{pan2010transfer}. The idea of holding on to learned structure
+has a long history, with roots in work on the role of forgetting
+\cite{markovitch1988role} and on the cost of reusing compiled knowledge chunks
+\cite{tambe1988some}.
+ It saves a fitted policy
+with its regime identity and retrieves the two together. The decision it makes is
+whether any adaptation is needed, and how to adapt is left aside. With no recurrence,
+the mechanism collapses into ordinary retraining.
+
 
 \section{Problem Formulation}
 
-Let a stream be a chronological sequence $x_1, x_2, \dots$, where
-$x_t \in \mathbb{R}^d$ is a feature vector and $y_t \in \{1,\dots,C\}$ its label,
-available only after the prediction for $x_t$. The stream is partitioned into
-fixed-size windows $W_1, \dots, W_T$, and window $W_k$ carries a regime identifier
-$r_k \in \mathcal{R}$. A regime is a semantically defined operating condition
-rather than a cluster label, such as a network scenario or a time-of-day block. A
-\emph{policy} $\pi$ is a fitted model with its decision rule, and the repository is a
-mapping
-$\mathcal{P}: \mathcal{R} \rightarrow \pi$ populated as regimes are encountered.
-When the stream moves from $r_{k-1}$ to $r_k$, the
-controller chooses $a_k \in \{\textsc{reuse}, \textsc{train}\}$: reuse
-$\mathcal{P}(r_k)$ if it exists, or train a new policy from the recent buffer. With
-$M(\pi, W)$ a performance measure (macro-F1 here) and $c(\pi)$ the cost of producing
-$\pi$ in adaptation CPU time, the problem is to keep $M(\cdot, W_k)$ close to a
-freshly trained policy's while minimizing $\sum_k c(a_k)$. Reuse assumes
-$P(Y \mid X, r_k)$ is unchanged since the policy was stored; if the regime identifier
-recurs but the conditional relationship has shifted, the stale policy is applied with
-no signal that it is stale. Distinguishing recurrence of the regime from recurrence
-of the label relationship is the central difficulty.
+
+We treat the data as a chronological stream $x_1, x_2, \dots$, where
+$x_t \in \mathbb{R}^d$ is a feature vector and $y_t \in \{1,\dots,C\}$ is its label. The
+label is unknown until the prediction for $x_t$ has been issued. The stream is divided
+into fixed-size windows $W_1, \dots, W_T$, and each window $W_k$ is tagged with a regime
+identifier $r_k \in \mathcal{R}$. A regime is a semantically defined operating
+condition, such as a network scenario or a time-of-day block. It is not a cluster label.
+A \emph{policy} $\pi$ is a fitted model plus its decision rule, and the repository is a
+mapping $\mathcal{P}: \mathcal{R} \rightarrow \pi$ that is filled in as new regimes show
+up. Whenever the stream moves from $r_{k-1}$ to $r_k$, the controller picks an action
+$a_k \in \{\textsc{reuse}, \textsc{train}\}$. It reuses $\mathcal{P}(r_k)$ when that
+exists, and trains a new policy on the recent buffer when it does not. Let $M(\pi, W)$
+be a performance measure, which is macro-F1 in this paper, and let $c(\pi)$ be the cost
+of producing $\pi$ in adaptation CPU time. The objective is to hold $M(\cdot, W_k)$ near
+the level a freshly trained policy would reach while making $\sum_k c(a_k)$ as small as
+possible.
+
+
+Reuse rests on an assumption, namely that $P(Y \mid X, r_k)$ has not changed since the
+policy was stored. If the regime identifier returns after the conditional relationship
+has shifted, the stale policy gets applied and nothing flags it. The hard part is
+separating a recurring regime from a recurring label relationship.
+
 
 \section{Proposed Method: RAPT}
+\label{sec:method}
+
 
 \subsection{Architecture and Repository}
-RAPT sits between the stream and the model and makes one decision per regime
-boundary. Each window is predicted with the currently active policy and evaluated
-once its labels are available, and the policy changes only at a regime transition or
-when monitoring reports degradation --- the prequential discipline of
-Section~\ref{sec:protocol}, which prevents a window's labels from influencing its own
-prediction. A regime is represented by its semantic identifier rather than a learned
-clustering, which keeps repository keys stable and avoids the failure mode in which
-clustering splits one operating condition into several keys and defeats reuse; the
-mechanism therefore depends on a usable regime signal, an assumption we revisit in
-Section~\ref{sec:limitations}. The repository stores, per regime, a checkpoint
-holding the fitted ensemble, its soft-voting weights, and creation and last-access
-timestamps. Checkpoints are immutable, so reuse retrieves the ensemble by reference
-rather than copying it, and only the small weight vector is adjusted when calibration
-applies; retrieval cost is therefore independent of model size, making reuse cheap
-relative to retraining.
+RAPT sits between the stream and the model and makes one decision at each regime
+boundary. The active policy predicts each window, and the window is scored when its
+labels arrive. A change of policy happens only at a regime transition or when
+monitoring reports degradation.
+
