+\subsection{Determinants of Cost and Trigger Behavior}
 \label{sec:cost}
-The cheap refresh was chosen from direct measurement. Holding the ensemble fixed and
-varying one factor at a time, a 20-tree refit cost approximately 22\,ms against
-104\,ms for 100 trees, and a 200-observation buffer approximately 80\,ms against
-104\,ms for 1000, so tree count dominated buffer size and reducing it from 100 to 20
-removed roughly four fifths of the refit cost. This is a property of the evaluated
-configuration; a different per-tree cost would shift the balance.
-Fig.~\ref{fig:tradeoff} places the RAPT variants and the baselines on the
-accuracy--cost plane for this stream.
+We determined the cheap refresh option through direct measurements. While holding the ensemble constant and varying each variable individually, we measured how long it took to refit when we changed factors such as tree count or buffer size. We saw that it takes around 22\,ms to refit a model with 20 trees and around 104\,ms to do the same with 100 trees. In addition, we observed that it takes around 80\,ms to fit a model when we use a 200-observation buffer and around 104\,ms when we use a 1000-observation buffer. Therefore, we can conclude that reducing the number of trees significantly reduces the time it takes to refit models. Specifically, we are able to reduce the refitting time by about four-fifths by reducing the number of trees used for modeling from 100 to 20. 
