-\subsection{Historical Detector Comparison}
-Three of the four detectors did not fire: ADWIN, Page-Hinkley and EDMA recorded zero
-adaptation events on all three streams and so reproduce the frozen baseline by
-construction. EDD fired 38--39 times per stream and reached the highest macro-F1 of
-any method on 5G Campus ($0.9847 \pm 0.0019$) and NordicDat ($0.4184 \pm 0.0165$),
-at the highest adaptation cost ($3.98$, $6.10$ and $4.69$\,s). Fig.~\ref{fig:detector}
-shows the three operating points. RAPT occupied a different point: 2,
-11 and 2 adaptation events at $0.2292$, $1.9711$ and $0.2594$\,s. It was cheaper
-than EDD on all three streams and more accurate on none, so EDD buys accuracy with
-adaptation events while RAPT sits at the lowest adaptation cost among methods that
-adapt.
+In section ~\ref{sec:ablation}, we established that RAPT-Evidence generates no refreshes. An example of a relative trigger asks if the current accuracy value is at least some threshold below a degraded reference built upon the prior accuracy values generated from the prior policy. The reference will degrade towards a poor level, and since this threshold is never met, the trigger will remain silent until the policy is no longer suitable. Compared to the threshold-based floor detector, which triggered 7.6 refreshes on data from the campus stream, RAPT-Floor generated no refreshes based on the relative rule.
+
