+Re-training is generally how to deal with ``drift'', however, when things go back to normal,
+a portion of what you pay for is probably in your previous trained model. RAPT retains
+the policies learned for each regime and pulls them out again if/when that regime comes
+back. It will only train anew when there isn't a suitable policy available and it will
+refresh a stored policy as soon as monitoring indicates that it doesn't fit anymore.
+On four network streams, our experiments showed that adapting CPU usage was 48--92\%
+lower compared to using full re-train. However, macro F-1 was lower on all four streams.
+Where regimes were going to occur again with different label relations (ie. same concepts,
+different labels), the blind reuse resulted in substantial damage; however, the parity-refit mechanism was able to fix this. We believe policy reuse is something to be deployed
+in cases where regimes recur with consistent or monitored label meanings. One thing we see
+as the next logical step in terms of adaptive reuse decisions, would be to test whether
+a retrieved regime has the same meaning as it did when the policy was stored.
